## Cross-consistency pass

The new section states both a capability and its limits, so they were read
against each other rather than only against the source:

- *"the committed markdown and the ref come out of one command"* vs *"the file is
  written, not staged"* — not contradictory, and the second is stated in the same
  section so the first cannot be read as "capture commits it for you". The
  sentence spells out the consequence: until you stage it, `retro_file` names a
  working-tree path that is not in history.
- *"the file name is the ref name"* vs the in-place case — the first is scoped to
  the outside-the-dir case in the bullet where it appears, and the very next
  paragraph says `retro_file` is **not always** `<retro-id>.md`. This is the
  contradiction the plan gate warned about (optional 2); it is now impossible to
  read only the strong half.
- *"exits non-zero"* vs *"the ref has been written"* — stated together in one
  bullet, with the ordering rationale, so a reader cannot conclude a non-zero exit
  means nothing happened.
- `docs/retro.md` says nothing detects a missing ref; `retro-ledger.md` says the
  same and names `etude-24d`. The two agree.

